# MPLS label withdraw: BGP labeled unicast control-plane exchange
# Spec: plan/spec-mpls-1-kernel.md AC-2
#
# Scope: this test exercises the BGP labeled-unicast control plane -- a peer
# announces then withdraws a labeled unicast prefix and ze accepts/re-encodes
# both (the expect=bgp lines below). The kernel data-plane outcome (the MPLS
# route removed from the kernel on withdraw) is verified separately, against a
# live kernel, by TestMPLSIntegration_Push (withdraw half) in
# internal/plugins/fib/kernel/mplsentry_integration_linux_test.go (//go:build
# integration && linux). Splitting it this way keeps the .ci harness free of an
# MPLS-capable kernel dependency while still proving end-to-end removal under QEMU.

option=skip-os:value=darwin
# ze-peer expectations
stdin=peer:terminator=EOF_PEER
option=asn:value=65002
option=open:value=add-capability:code=1:hex=00010004
# `cmd=api:...:text=` is a REPORT LABEL, not an executed command: parseCmd stores
# it on the message as msg.Cmd and report.go is its only other reader
# (record_parse.go:825). Both lines here read like real API calls -- and they are
# exactly the strings ze_api.API.send() would route to
# ze-plugin-engine:update-route -- but nothing ever sent them, so ze announced
# nothing, withdrew nothing, and the test timed out waiting for message 1. The
# route-driver plugin below now issues them for real, in order.
#
# The announce is driven from the plugin rather than declared in the peer's
# `update` block (the form mpls-push.ci uses) precisely BECAUSE this test also
# withdraws: a config-declared announce races the plugin's withdraw, and the
# withdrawal of a route that has not reached the wire yet proves nothing.
cmd=api:conn=1:seq=1:text=route-driver announces the labeled prefix
# LOCAL_PREF is ABSENT on purpose. RFC 4271 Section 5.1.5 confines LOCAL_PREF to
# IBGP, and this peer is eBGP (65001 -> 65002). Commit d33033872 made the sender
# strip it and left this expectation encoding the old, non-conformant output; the
# hex below is what ze now sends, corrected 2026-08-02. A test that pins a
# violation is the violation with a green bar on top (ai/rules/rfc-compliance.md).
expect=bgp:conn=1:seq=1:hex=FFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF003702000000204001010040020602010000FDE9800E10000104040A0000020030003E810A0100
cmd=api:conn=1:seq=2:text=route-driver withdraws it
expect=bgp:conn=1:seq=2:hex=FFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF0024020000000D800F0A00010430003E810A0100
EOF_PEER

# Native route driver announces, waits for the wire counter, then withdraws.

# ze bgp configuration
stdin=ze-bgp:terminator=EOF_CONF
plugin {
	external route-driver {
		run "ze-test fixture plugin/mpls-withdraw"
		encoder json
	}
}

bgp {
	peer peer1 {
		connection {
			remote {
				ip 127.0.0.1
			}
			local {
				ip 127.0.0.1
				accept false
			}
		}
		session {
			asn {
				local 65001
				remote 65002
			}
			router-id 1.2.3.4
			family {
				ipv4/mpls-label { prefix { maximum 10000; } }
			}
		}
		behavior {
			group-updates disable
		}

		attach process route-driver {
			send [ update ]
		}
	}
}
EOF_CONF

# Process orchestration
cmd=background:seq=1:exec=ze-peer --port $PORT:stdin=peer
cmd=foreground:seq=2:exec=ze -:stdin=ze-bgp:timeout=60s
